From 66e57766dccd98b2dbc214cf3edff7483ef41c07 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: David Do <david.do@openkingdom.com>
Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 11:27:00 +0400
Subject: [PATCH 27/27] 9chain-a1: the validator barrier is 81 LOVE9, because
 25,000 was a door nobody could walk through

MinValidatorStake was 25,000 LOVE9, described in its own comment as "deliberately low so the
community can run its own node". Measured on G-day, hours before genesis: the faucet grants 10
LOVE9 per request and rate-limits to 5 per IP per hour, so reaching 25,000 is about 500 hours of
uninterrupted asking. There is no other documented path. That is not a slow road, it is no road.

This value is compiled into the binary -- netgen never writes minValidatorStake into genesis.json
(measured: zero hits across every generated net*/genesis.json) -- so it is immutable for the life
of the network. Shipping it would have permanently closed the door this network exists to hold
open, and the last moment it could be changed cheaply is before genesis.

81 = 9 x 9: nine requests from a faucet granting 9. Avalanche's own Fuji uses 1 * units.Avax, so
81 is still 81x that. Safety is untouched: the nine genesis validators self-bond 8,999,991 LOVE9,
so an 81-LOVE9 validator holds ~0.0009% of stake and cannot influence consensus; sybils gain
nothing because stake is what votes.

MinDelegatorStake moves with it, 312.5 -> 9. Leaving it would have made delegating 3.9x more
expensive than running your own validator, frozen in for the life of the network. The two
barriers express an ordering -- lending stake must never cost more than putting up a node -- and
9 < 81 keeps it.
